{
  "concept": "A rational graph can break either by having one removable missing point or by becoming unbounded at a vertical asymptote, and these come from different algebraic causes.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal x-axis with arrowheads and label x",
    "vertical y-axis with arrowheads and label y",
    "dotted gray coordinate grid",
    "x-axis tick marks labeled -3, -1, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9",
    "y-axis tick marks labeled -6, -4, -2, 2, 4, 6",
    "teal rational function left branch approaching the x-axis from above on the far left",
    "teal rational function left branch crossing the x-axis near x = 1 and falling toward negative infinity as x approaches 3 from the left",
    "teal rational function right branch rising from positive infinity just right of x = 3 and leveling toward y = 1 to the right",
    "teal open circle at x = -1 marking the removable discontinuity",
    "blue annotation text 'Removable discontinuity at x = -1'",
    "thin blue arrow from the removable discontinuity label to the open circle",
    "red dashed vertical line at x = 3",
    "red annotation text 'Vertical asymptote at x = 3'",
    "trace point on the curve controlled by x near the vertical asymptote",
    "small dynamic label showing the trace point's x-value and y-value",
    "optional algebra panel showing f(x) = ((x - 1)(x + 1)) / ((x - 3)(x + 1))",
    "optional algebra panel showing cancellation of the common factor x + 1",
    "optional algebra panel showing simplified form f(x) = (x - 1) / (x - 3), with x ≠ -1 and x ≠ 3"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "highlightBreak",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Highlight break type",
      "options": [
        "Removable discontinuity",
        "Vertical asymptote"
      ],
      "default": "Removable discontinuity",
      "teaches": "A removable discontinuity is a single missing point on an otherwise continuous branch, while a vertical asymptote is a place where the graph grows without bound"
    },
    {
      "id": "approachAsymptote",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Trace x near 3",
      "range": [
        2.1,
        3.9,
        0.05
      ],
      "default": 2.5,
      "teaches": "As x gets close to 3, the y-value shoots downward from the left and upward from the right, so the break at x = 3 cannot be repaired by filling in one point"
    },
    {
      "id": "factorCancelledView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Factor-cancelled view",
      "options": [
        "Graph only",
        "Show cancellation"
      ],
      "default": "Graph only",
      "teaches": "The hole at x = -1 comes from a cancelled factor, so the simplified curve is continuous there except that the original function still has one excluded input"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Find the removable hole",
      "narration": "The hollow circle at x = -1 marks a point that is missing from the graph. The nearby curve behaves normally on both sides, so this break is only one absent function value.",
      "state": {
        "highlightBreak": "Removable discontinuity",
        "approachAsymptote": 2.5,
        "factorCancelledView": "Graph only"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reveal why the hole occurs",
      "narration": "The common factor x + 1 cancels algebraically, leaving the same curve shape but preserving the restriction x ≠ -1. That is why the graph has a hole rather than a vertical blow-up at x = -1.",
      "state": {
        "highlightBreak": "Removable discontinuity",
        "approachAsymptote": 2.5,
        "factorCancelledView": "Show cancellation"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Contrast the vertical asymptote",
      "narration": "At x = 3, the dashed red line is not a missing point. Moving the trace point toward x = 3 shows the curve plunging downward on the left and rising upward on the right.",
      "state": {
        "highlightBreak": "Vertical asymptote",
        "approachAsymptote": 2.9,
        "factorCancelledView": "Show cancellation"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approach from the right",
      "narration": "Just to the right of x = 3, the graph is extremely high and then falls back toward its long-run level near y = 1. This unbounded behavior is what makes x = 3 a vertical asymptote.",
      "state": {
        "highlightBreak": "Vertical asymptote",
        "approachAsymptote": 3.1,
        "factorCancelledView": "Show cancellation"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A cancelled factor creates a removable hole, but an uncancelled denominator zero creates a vertical asymptote where the function values become unbounded.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate a square coordinate grid with black arrowed axes, teal curve, teal open circle at approximately (-1, 0.5), and a red dashed vertical line at x = 3. The curve should resemble f(x) = ((x - 1)(x + 1)) / ((x - 3)(x + 1)), simplified to (x - 1)/(x - 3) except for the hole at x = -1: it crosses the x-axis near x = 1, drops to negative infinity as x approaches 3 from the left, rises from positive infinity just right of x = 3, and approaches horizontal level y = 1 on the right. Use blue styling for the removable discontinuity annotation and red styling for the vertical asymptote annotation. Highlighting should brighten or thicken the selected feature while dimming the other; the slider should place a trace point on the curve unless x is exactly 3, where it should show an undefined warning rather than plotting a finite point."
}

